---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi["case-saturday"];
const rate = { before: 4, after: 6 };
const sugar = { still: 60, stirred: 15 };

type Arm = { label: string; factors: [string, string, boolean][] };
const designs: { field: string; confounded: Arm[]; controlled: Arm[]; result: string }[] = [
  {
    field: "Two ways to test the headline",
    confounded: [
      { label: `Last week · ${rate.before}%`, factors: [["Headline", "old", false], ["Button", "blue", false], ["Visitors", "last week", false]] },
      { label: `This week · ${rate.after}%`, factors: [["Headline", "new", true], ["Button", "green", true], ["Visitors", "this week", true]] },
    ],
    controlled: [
      { label: "Version A", factors: [["Headline", "old", false], ["Button", "same", false], ["Visitors", "random half", false]] },
      { label: "Version B", factors: [["Headline", "new", true], ["Button", "same", false], ["Visitors", "random half", false]] },
    ],
    result: `+${rate.after - rate.before} points observed`,
  },
];

const trials = [
  { id: "both", label: "Change both", water: true, stir: true },
  { id: "water", label: "Change the water only", water: true, stir: false },
  { id: "stir", label: "Change the stirring only", water: false, stir: true },
  { id: "none", label: "Change neither", water: false, stir: false },
];
const estimate = (t: { water: boolean; stir: boolean }): [string, string, string] =>
  t.water && t.stir ? ["Confounded", "is-bad", "Temperature and stirring changed together. The difference has two possible sources."]
  : t.water ? ["Temperature", "is-ok", "Only the water temperature differs, so the difference estimates its effect."]
  : t.stir ? ["Stirring", "is-ok", "Only the stirring differs, so the difference estimates its effect."]
  : ["Nothing", "is-quiet", "Nothing differs between the trials, so any gap is run-to-run variation."];

const rows = [["A", 30], ["B", 20], ["B", 20]] as const;
const buggy = rows.reduce((n, [, a]) => n + a, 0);
const seen = new Set<string>();
const fixed = rows.reduce((n, [id, a]) => (seen.has(id) ? n : (seen.add(id), n + a)), 0);

const checks = [
  ["Two changes", "Sign-ups rise after you change both the headline and the button color. What can you conclude from that comparison, and what remains unknown?"],
  ["Redesign", "How would you set up the sugar experiment to isolate the effect of stirring?"],
  ["The pipeline", "Why is running the fixed pipeline on the original three rows a better check of this bug fix than comparing the old total with a total from fresh data?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("case-saturday")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's controlled-comparison study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 3 and classify two more comparisons as one change or two: Tuesday's table against a replay with a new alias list, and against a re-fetch with that same new list."
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">One change at a time</h2>
  <p class="pr-pull">A controlled comparison changes one input and holds the other relevant conditions steady.</p>
  <p>If the outcome changes, the setup gives you a reason to attribute the difference to the input you changed. If you change two inputs together, you can measure their combined effect, but you can’t tell how much each one contributed.</p>

  <h2>1. Website A/B test</h2>
  <p>Suppose {rate.before}% of visitors signed up last week. This week you rewrote the headline and changed the sign-up button from blue to green. Now {rate.after}% sign up. The increase is <strong>{rate.after - rate.before} percentage points</strong>, but the headline and button color are <strong>confounded</strong>. They changed together, so the increase could come from either change or from the two working together. The mix of visitors might also have changed from one week to the next.</p>
  <p>To isolate the headline’s effect, randomly assign visitors during the same period to two versions. Both versions use the same button, layout, and sign-up flow. One shows the old headline and the other shows the new one. Random assignment helps balance differences among visitors, and running both versions at the same time keeps them exposed to the same day-to-day conditions. Any systematic difference in sign-up rates can then be attributed to the headline, with some uncertainty from random variation.</p>

  <figure class="pr-card pa-designs">
    {designs.map((d) => (
      <div>
        <p class="pr-label">{d.field}</p>
        <div class="pa-pair">
          {([["Before and after", d.confounded, d.result, "is-bad"], ["Controlled", d.controlled, "One change", "is-ok"]] as const).map(([name, arms, verdict, tone]) => (
            <div class="pa-side">
              <p class="pa-side-head"><b>{name}</b><span class:list={["pr-chip", tone]}>{verdict}</span></p>
              {arms.map((arm) => (
                <div class="pa-arm">
                  <span>{arm.label}</span>
                  <ul>{arm.factors.map(([k, v, changed]) => <li class={changed ? "is-changed" : ""}>{k}: {v}</li>)}</ul>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
    <p class="pr-legend"><span class="pa-key">Differs between the two arms</span></p>
    <figcaption>The before and after changed the visitors too, so its rise is an observed difference, not the combined effect of the two edits. Only the controlled version measures an effect.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>2. Laboratory experiment</h2>
  <p>Suppose sugar dissolves in {sugar.still} seconds in cool, still water and in {sugar.stirred} seconds in warm, stirred water. Temperature and stirring changed together. Those two measurements can’t tell you how much of the {sugar.still - sugar.stirred}-second difference came from warming the water.</p>
  <p>To isolate temperature, compare cool and warm water with no stirring in either trial. Use the same amount and grain size of sugar, the same volume of water, identical containers, and the same rule for deciding when the sugar has dissolved. Repeat each condition to see how much the times vary.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-trial" aria-live="polite">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>Design the second trial</legend>
      {trials.map((t, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-conf-trial" value={t.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{t.label}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    {trials.map((t) => {
      const [what, tone, why] = estimate(t);
      return (
        <div class="pa-trial-view" data-trial={t.id}>
          <div class="pa-pair">
            <div class="pa-arm"><span>Trial 1</span><ul><li>Water: cool</li><li>Stirring: none</li></ul></div>
            <div class="pa-arm"><span>Trial 2</span><ul><li class={t.water ? "is-changed" : ""}>Water: {t.water ? "warm" : "cool"}</li><li class={t.stir ? "is-changed" : ""}>Stirring: {t.stir ? "stirred" : "none"}</li></ul></div>
          </div>
          <p class="pa-trial-say"><span class="pa-trial-k">Measures</span><span class:list={["pr-chip", tone]}>{what}</span> {why}</p>
        </div>
      );
    })}
  </figure>

  <h2>3. Fixing a data pipeline</h2>
  <p>Suppose a pipeline is meant to sum payments by transaction ID, counting each transaction once. Its input contains three rows:</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-pipe">
    <table class="pr-table pr-num">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Transaction ID</th><th scope="col">Amount</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map(([id, a]) => <tr><th scope="row">{id}</th><td>${a}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="pa-pipe-out">
      <p><span>Buggy code, these rows</span><b>${buggy}</b></p>
      <p class="is-fixed"><span>Fixed code, the same rows</span><b>${fixed}</b></p>
      <p class="pa-pipe-diff">−${buggy - fixed}: one input changed, so one cause</p>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <p>The buggy code counts all three rows and reports <strong>${buggy}</strong>. The fixed code runs on the <strong>same rows</strong> and reports <strong>${fixed}</strong>, the expected total. Because the input stayed fixed, you can trace the ${buggy - fixed} decrease to the code change. If you ran the fixed code only on freshly collected data and got ${fixed}, that difference could also reflect a different set of payments. The original input lets you check whether the fix corrects the specific failure you saw.</p>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>Case 1 makes the reply's two comparisons from three tables. Table A, Tuesday's table, is the baseline. A against B is the controlled comparison, like the reply's three-row replay: Friday's saved pages held fixed and only the tidy rule changed, so every difference has one cause. A against C is the before and after: the code and the pages changed together.</p>
    <p>An honest changelog line is written the way the reply writes its last paragraph. It names the one input that changed, and claims nothing that input can't explain.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>4. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-trial .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-trial-view { display: none; }
  .pa-trial:has([value="both"]:checked) [data-trial="both"],
  .pa-trial:has([value="water"]:checked) [data-trial="water"],
  .pa-trial:has([value="stir"]:checked) [data-trial="stir"],
  .pa-trial:has([value="none"]:checked) [data-trial="none"] { display: block; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-trial-say { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-trial-k { margin-right: 0.4rem; color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .pa-trial :global(.pr-chip.is-quiet) { border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); background: transparent; color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-designs { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
  .pa-pair { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .pa-side { display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; align-content: start; padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-side-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.3rem; margin: 0; }
  .pa-side-head b { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  .pa-arm { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 9.5rem) 1fr; gap: 0.5rem; align-items: center; font-size: 0.86rem; }
  .pa-arm > span { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-arm ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.3rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .pa-arm li { padding: 0.05rem 0.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 999px; background: var(--tl-card); white-space: nowrap; }
  .pa-arm li.is-changed { border-color: #d9822b; background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 18%, var(--tl-card)); color: var(--sl-color-white); font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-key::before { border: 1px solid #d9822b; background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 30%, transparent) !important; border-radius: 3px !important; }
  .pa-pipe { display: grid; gap: 0.8rem; align-items: center; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-pipe { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .pa-pipe-out { display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-pipe-out p { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0; padding: 0.45rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-card); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-pipe-out p.is-fixed { border-color: var(--pr-green); }
  .pa-pipe-out b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.3rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-pipe-out .pa-pipe-diff { border: 0; background: transparent; color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.84rem; }
</style>
